{"parentUuid":"9b9f5da1-962b-40b0-abd3-733d90a1b9b2","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_0113Bbs6pjJpNUUCimx68bJg","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now let me understand the theorem:\n\n`bvor_slt`: `~(s - t) | s <s t ↔ ∃x. x | s <s t`\n\nThe forward direction (→): If `~(s - t) | s <s t`, then we can witness with `x = ~(s - t)`.\nThe backward direction (←): If `∃x. x | s <s t`, then `~(s - t) | s <s t`.\n\nFor the backward direction, we need to show that `~(s - t) | s` is a \"maximum\" in some sense - that for any x, if `x | s <s t`, then `~(s - t) | s <s t` too. \n\n\nThe forward direction is straightforward—I just witness with `x = ~(s - t)`. For the backward direction, if there exists any `x` where `x | s <s t`, I need to prove that `~(s - t) | s <s t` holds, which suggests that `~(s - t) | s` represents some kind of maximal value that the bitwise OR can produce.\n\nBut wait, that's counterintuitive since bitwise OR only increases values in unsigned arithmetic, so `x | s ≥ s` always. The signed comparison complicates things though—two's complement means the ordering behaves differently. Let me look at how similar theorems like `bvshr_slt` handle this pattern to understand the right approach.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4560,"cache_read_input_tokens":18271,"output_tokens":842,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4560,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":842,"cache_read_input_tokens":18271,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4560,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4560},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null},"requestId":"req_011CaRBhN1w6SykKLcjc4EAw","type":"assista