se = false -> bv_slt a b = true.\nProof. intros.\n        unfold bv_slt.\n        rewrite H, N.eqb_refl.\n        apply slt_list_be_ft with (d := false).\n        - Reconstr.reasy (@Coq.NArith.Nnat.Nat2N.id, \n             @Coq.Lists.List.length_rev) \n            (@RAWBITVECTOR_LIST.size, @RAWBITVECTOR_LIST.bitvector).\n        - now rewrite hd_rev.\n        - now rewrite hd_rev.\nQed.\n\nLemma bv_slt_false_zeros: forall a, bv_slt a (zeros (size a)) = false -> \n  eqb (last a false) false = true.\nProof. intros.\n        unfold bv_slt in H. \n        rewrite zeros_size, N.eqb_refl in H.\n        unfold slt_list in H.\n        induction a using rev_ind; intros.\n        - now cbn.\n        - cbn in *. case_eq x; intros.\n          + subst. assert ((rev (a ++ [true])) = (true :: rev a)).\n            Reconstr.reasy (@Coq.Lists.List.rev_unit) Reconstr.Empty.\n            rewrite H0 in H.\n            assert ((rev (zeros (size (a ++ [true])))) =  (zeros (size (a ++ [true])))).\n            Reconstr.reasy (@RAWBITVECTOR_LIST.rev_mk_list_false) (@RAWBITVECTOR_LIST.zeros, @RAWBITVECTOR_LIST.bitvector).\n            rewrite slt_list_be_ft with (d := false) in H.\n            easy.","numLines":30,"startLine":8229,"totalLines":15615}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"fb69c821-8f2a-4678-aaf2-b2c2b5615942","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-vscode","cwd":"/home/hakull245/bitvector-vibe-proving","sessionId":"2b65148c-b4a0-40d0-9fba-835c4508523c","version":"2.1.120","gitBranch":"haVibeProving"}
{"parentUuid":"9488a1e6-a3fc-4cc1-a5be-4c19b9dc6ebb","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_016dsgdpXJg3TwzHdoBCtgvF","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have a clearer picture. Let me now look at the `last_true_list2int_lb` lemma and think about how to structure the proof.\n\nOK, I think I've been overthinking this. Let me try a different approach - writing the proof in a more abstract way.\n\nThe key insight I've been using: in Case A (bv_ult s t), the bound `bv2int(v|s) <= bv2int t - 1` holds.\n\nLet me structure the proof as follows:\n\nFor the backward direction of `bvor_slt`:\n\n\nGiven the existence of some value satisfying the condition, I need to show `bv_slt (v|s) t = true` where `v = ~(s-t)`. I'll split on whether the last bit of t is false (meaning t is nonnegative), then further case on whether the last bit of s is false, and within that analyze whether s is less than t in the unsigned sense to establish the required signed comparison.\n\nNow for the case where s ≥ t unsigned: I need to determine the last bit of v|s by computing the last bit of s-t. Since both s and t are nonnegative (last bits false), the subtraction s-t doesn't borrow, so bv2int(s-t) = bv2int s - bv2int t. Since bv2int s < 2^(n-1), this difference is also less than 2^(n-1), meaning the last bit of s-t is false, and therefore the last bit of v is true.\n\nWhen s is negative (last bit true), v|s becomes negative regardless of v, and since t is nonnegative, the signed less-than comparison correctly returns true. For the case where t is also negative, I'm checking whether the unsigned comparison s ≥ t holds, which determines whether the signed comparison between x|s and t is satisfied. I'm working through the case where the sign bit of s is false, examining subcases based on whether v is positive or negative. When v is positive, the result is negative and the comparison holds, but when v is negative, I need to verify that the bitwise OR produces a negative result to satisfy the signed less-than condition, which requires checking that the combined value stays below t's integer representation. to show this):\n    last v = false means bv2int v < 2^(n-1), i.e., bv2int t - bv2int s - 1 < 2^(n-1), i.e., bv2int t <= 2^(n-1) + bv2int s.\n    So bv2int(x|s) >= 2^(n-1) + bv2int s >= bv2int t. Contradiction. ✓\n    \n  - Not Case A (s >=u t): bv2int s >= bv2int t. With last s = false: bv2int s < 2^(n-1). With last t = true: bv2int t >= 2^(n-1). Contradiction (bv2int s < 2^(n-1) <= bv2int t but also >= bv2int t). So NOT Case A is impossible here\n\nWhen last s = false and last t = true, I get bv2int s < 2^(n-1) <= bv2int t, which means bv_ult s t must be true, so the \"not Case A\" scenario is vacuously satisfied. Now I can organize the proof by the four combinations of last t and last s: when both are false, I split on bv_ult s t; when t is false and s is true, it's the bv_slt_tf case; when both are true, I use the hypothesis to deduce bv_ult s t and apply arithmetic; and when t is true and s is false, I further split on last v to handle the remaining subcases. I'm working through the key identities needed: applying the bitwise operation lemma to x and ~s gives me a relationship between the AND and OR operations, and I can express the bitwise complement in terms of the integer value to complete the chain of equalities. Verifying the bitwise identities: when I split x into parts masked by s and its complement, the intersection should be zero and the union should reconstruct x. Each bit position confirms this algebraically, but I need to find or prove the corresponding Coq lemmas for bitvector operations to formalize this reasoning.\n\nActually, I'm taking a step back—there's a simpler approach using the existing `last_true_list2int_lb` lemma, which gives me a lower bound when the last bit is true. Since the bitwise OR of x and s will have its last bit set to true whenever x's last bit is true, I can directly apply this to bound `bv2int(x|s)` from below.\n\nFor case 4b specifically, I need to work with the constraint that `bv2int v = bv2int t - bv2int s - 1` when last s is false and last t is true, combined with the bounds on t and s to derive the necessary inequality.\n\nNow looking at the signed less-than condition: since `bv_slt(x|s, t)` must hold and t is negative, x|s also has to be negative (otherwise a nonnegative value would be greater than a negative one in signed comparison). This means last x must be true since last s is false. From here I can use the fact that both values are negative to compare their integer representations directly: `bv2int(x|s) < bv2int t`, and I also know `bv2int(x|s) >= bv2int s` from the bitwise OR property.\n\nBut I'm realizing there's a subtlety with the endianness—the lemma I was referencing works on big-endian lists, while the actual bitvectors here are little-endian, so the comparison operations use a reversed version. Let me look for a direct lemma about the lower bound property of bitwise OR that applies to this representation. I'm verifying that `ule_list_big_endian_or` establishes the relationship between `s` and `s|x` by showing `s <=u x|s` through commutativity of OR, then using `ule_list_list2int` to convert this to an inequality on the integer values: `bv2int(x|s) >= bv2int s`.\n\nBut this bound alone isn't strong enough—I need `bv2int(x|s) >= 2^(n-1) + bv2int s`. The challenge is that I have two separate constraints: `bv2int(x|s) >= 2^(n-1)` from the high bit being set, and `bv2int(x|s) >= bv2int s` from the OR property, but combining them with max doesn't yield the sum I need.\n\nLet me try a different approach using the identity `bv2int(x|s) = bv2int x + bv2int s - bv2int(x & s)`. Since `bv2int x >= 2^(n-1)` and `bv2int(x & s) <= bv2int s`, I can work with this decomposition to get the bound I'm looking for. So the last bit of `x & s` is false since the last bit of `s` is false, meaning `bv2int(x & s) < 2^(n-1)`. Since each bit of `x & s` is componentwise at most the corresponding bit of `s`, I have `bv2int(x & s) <= bv2int s`.\n\nNow for the OR: `bv2int(x|s) = bv2int x + bv2int s - bv2int(x & s)`, which is at least `2^(n-1)` since `bv2int x >= 2^(n-1)`. But I actually need `bv2int(x|s) >= 2^(n-1) + bv2int s`, which would require `bv2int x - bv2int(x & s) >= 2^(n-1)`.\n\nGiven that the last bit of x is true and the last bit of s is false, the last bit of their AND is false, so `bv2int(x & s) < 2^(n-1)`. Since `bv2int x >= 2^(n-1)`, I need `bv2int(x & s) <= bv2int x - 2^(n-1)`. But this doesn't hold in general—`bv2int(x & s)` could range from 0 to `2^(n-1) - 1` while `bv2int x - 2^(n-1)` could be much smaller, so I can construct a counterexample.\n\nLet me test some specific cases: with n=3, x=110 (value 6) and s=010 (value 2), the AND gives 010 (value 2), and 6 - 4 = 2, so the inequality holds exactly. But trying another case with x=111 (value 7) and s=011 (value 3)...\n\nThe AND gives 011 (value 3), and 7 - 4 = 3, so again it's equal. Testing x=101 (value 5) with the same s gives 001 (value 1), and 5 - 4 = 1, which also checks out. I'm starting to see a pattern here—when the last bit of x is 1 and the last bit of s is 0, the bitwise AND seems to always satisfy this inequality. Let me work through the mathematical proof by expanding what bv2int actually computes.\n\nSince x has its last bit set to 1, I can write its value as 2^(n-1) plus the sum of the lower bits. For the AND operation, since s has its last bit as 0, the result has 0 in that position too. So the AND only involves the lower n-1 bits, and I need to show that each individual bit position in the AND is bounded by the corresponding bit in x.\n\nThis means bv2int(x & s) is at most bv2int(x) minus 2^(n-1)—the key insight being that the most significant bit contributes 2^(n-1) to x's value but contributes nothing to the AND result since s's MSB is 0. From here I can use the inclusion-exclusion principle: bv2int(x|s) equals bv2int(x) plus bv2int(s) minus bv2int(x & s). >= bv2int x + bv2int s - (bv2int x - 2^(n-1)) = 2^(n-1) + bv2int s. ✓\n\nAnd this follows from: bv2int(x & s) <= bv2int x - 2^(n-1) (because last s = false means last(x & s) = false, so bv2int(x & s) is the sum without the MSB term, and bv2int x = 2^(n-1) + ..., so bv2int x - 2^(n-1) = sum of lower bits).\n\nMore formally: `bv2int(x & s) <= bv2int x - 2^(length x - 1)` when `last s false = false` and `last x false = true`. This can be rephrased as `bv2int(x & s) + 2^(n-1) <= bv2int x`.\n\nNow I'm looking for an existing lemma that captures this relationship. There's `last_false_and_true_submask_bound` which seems close but goes in the opposite direction—it states that when a has last bit false and b has last bit true, then the bitwise AND of a and b is bounded by b minus the MSB contribution.\n\nBy commutativity of AND, I can swap the roles: if I apply this lemma with s and x swapped (where s has last bit false and x has last bit true), I get exactly what I need: `bv2int(x & s) <= bv2int x - 2^(n-1)`.\n\nNow I can use this bound in the OR formula. Since `bv2int(x|s) = bv2int x + bv2int s - bv2int(x & s)`, substituting the upper bound on the AND term gives me `bv2int(x|s) >= 2^(n-1) + bv2int s`, which is what case 4b requires.\n\nI need to find the lemma that establishes AND is commutative at the bitvector level—it should follow from the fact that the underlying AND operation on lists is commutative. I need to find whether there's a direct lemma for `map2 andb` commutativity, or if I need to derive it from the pointwise commutativity of `andb` itself.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":706,"cache_read_input_tokens":138495,"output_tokens":7375,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":706,"ephemeral