{"parentUuid":"0c8a3771-08bd-48cf-9acf-ccf1a876cf8a","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_01MGM3RMaLYMGdrxWjHCbQRU","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me look at the four theorems and pick the simplest one to start with. \n\n1. `bvmult_slt`: `∃x. x*s <s t ↔ ~(neg t) & (neg s | s) <s t`\n   - The IC is `bv_and (bv_not (bv_neg t)) (bv_or (bv_neg s) s) <s t`\n   - Note: `bv_or (bv_neg s) s` — when s=0, this is 0; when s≠0, this gives all bits set (I think `-s | s` has all bits set when s≠0, i.e., it equals `0xFFFF...` or something). \n\nNow I'm working through the two's complement arithmetic for `-s | s`. In two's complement, `-s = ~s + 1`, so `-s | s = ~s + 1 | s`, which gets complicated. For the right side, `~(-t) = t - 1` in two's complement, so I'm looking at `(t-1) & (-s | s) <s t`.\n\n2. `bvmult_sgt`: `∃x. x*s >s t ↔ t <s t - ((s|t) | -s)`\n   - The IC involves `bv_subt t (` and continues from there.\n\n3. `bvmult_sle`: `∃x. x*s ≤s t ↔ ~(s=0 ∧ t <s s)`\n   - This one has a logical/propositional RHS rather than a bitvector expression, so it might have a different structure.\n\n4. `bvmult_sge`: `∃x. x*s ≥s t ↔ (-s | s) & max_s ≥s t`\n   - I need to use `bv_sge_iff_sle` lemma and possibly reduce to `bv_slt`. The expression `(-s | s) & signed_max n ≥s t` involves `signed_max n`, which is the most positive value like `0x7FFF...`.\n\nThe `signed_max` operation zeros out the MSB when combined with `(-s | s)`. Looking at the structural complexity, `bvmult_sle` seems like the simplest starting point since it has a propositional RHS that I can handle with a case split on whether s equals zero. For the forward direction, when s is zero, x*s equals zero for all x, so I just need to verify that zero is less than or equal to t, which holds when t's most significant bit is zero.\n\nNow I'm reconsidering the RHS condition. The theorem states the bitvector IC is `~(s=0 ∧ bv_slt t s = true)`, which fails only when s is zero and t is negative. This means when s is zero and t is negative, there's no x satisfying x*0 ≤s t, but when s is zero and t is non-negative, any x works.\n\nFor non-zero s, any t is valid since we can construct x = t/s in modular arithmetic. The `bvmult_sle` IC is simpler as a negated logical condition rather than a complex bitvector expression, though the `~` notation in a Coq bitvector proof context might be tricky. I think `bvmult_slt` is the best starting point since it has a similar structure to `bvor_slt` and we already have conversion lemmas like `bv_sge_iff_sle` available.\n\nLooking at the specific comments for each lemma, `bvmult_slt` appears cleanest without additional complexity, while `bvmult_sgt`, `bvmult_sle`, and `bvmult_sge` each have their own IC conditions involving signed comparisons and bitwise operations. I'll go with `bvmult_slt` as the current problem to work on.\n\nActually, let me reconsider which one is most tractable. `bvmult_sle` has an unusual propositional structure on the right side, so I'll skip that for now. `bvmult_sge` involves several operations including negation and bitwise OR, and I'm trying to understand the property of `(-s | s)` — in two's complement, `-s = ~s + 1`, so when s is nonzero, the bitwise OR of s and its negation should have interesting properties worth exploring. I'm not confident in that analysis, so let me just focus on `bvmult_slt` since it's the first one to tackle. I'll update CLAUDE.md to mark this as the current open problem.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1054,"cache_read_input_tokens":85411,"output_tokens":2237,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1054,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":2237,"cache_read_input_toke