{"parentUuid":"450cc77b-9d6c-4138-9c5e-09a797750b74","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_01SydYWaPvqV2iJzx8AnwoZ8","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"WSL is the most likely culprit then. A few known ways WSL can silently lose files in the Linux filesystem (`ext4.vhdx`):\n\n1. **Windows shutdown/restart while WSL was running** — if WSL didn't flush cleanly, recent writes to the virtual disk can be lost. `.jsonl` files are written incrementally during a session, so a dirty shutdown drops whatever wasn't flushed.\n\n2. **`wsl --shutdown` or Windows update** — same effect, especially if a session was active at the time.\n\n3. **The `ext4.vhdx` was corrupted and auto-repaired** — WSL2 runs on a virtual disk and occasionally repairs itself on mount, which can delete files it considers inconsistent.\n\n4. **Disk space** — if the Windows host ran out of disk space, WSL writes fail silently and partial files can disappear.\n\nUnfortunately none of these are recoverable after the fact. Going forward, the only real protection is periodically backing up `~/.claude/projects/` to a Windows-side path (e.g. `/mnt/c/Users/...`) since Windows-side files survive WSL issues:\n\n```bash\ncp -r ~/.claude/projects /mnt/c/Users/<you>/claude-backup\n```"}],"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":245,"cache_read_input_tokens":94601,"output_tokens":294,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":245,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":3,"output_tokens":294,"cache_read_input_tokens":94601,"cache_creation_input_tokens":245,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":245},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null},"requestId":"req_011Cbkc1ygsuSs6xnioVT